EXT. RUINED WINDMILL - MINEFIELD - DAWN

A stone tower, sails broken and hanging. A field of tall grass ringed with rusted wire. Fog clings to the ground. A crow lands on the wire and shakes itself.

SERGEANT PETER GARRITY, 40s, a bandaged ear, crouches in the mill doorway with a rifle. LUCA FERRANTE, 20s, a radioman, hunches beside him, dials tuning, mouth open. ANSELM VOGT, 30s, a sapper with a prod and a headlamp, kneels in the grass ten yards out, probing the soil with infinite care.

GARRITY
Anselm. Talk to me.

ANSELM
Clear for two meters.

GARRITY
Move.

ANSELM
Two meters. Don't rush me.

He edges forward. Prods. Prods. The grass whispers. Luca checks the radio, jiggles a knob, curses.

LUCA
No signal.

GARRITY
Keep trying.

LUCA
It's the fog.

GARRITY
It's always the fog.

Anselm stops. His prod hits something. A soft metallic knock. He freezes. Garrity and Luca freeze too. A breath held by three men.

ANSELM
(very quietly)
Contact.

GARRITY
Type?

ANSELM
Bounding. S-mine.

GARRITY
Leave it.

ANSELM
I'm going around.

GARRITY
Anselm.

ANSELM ^
I'm going around.

He shifts, belly down, wiggles past the mine, a hand's breadth at a time. Garrity watches. Luca crosses himself with the hand not holding the radio. Fog rolls across the field.

A SOUND. The crow bursts off the wire in a clatter of wings. Anselm flinches. His elbow lands on a loose stone, the stone rocks, and a bootlace pulls taut.

ANSELM (CONT'D)
No. No, no, no --

GARRITY
Down!

Garrity and Luca drop flat. Anselm throws himself backward. The mine leaps from the soil, a black can on a column of dust, and detonates in the air with a crack that tears the dawn.

Silence. Ringing. Smoke. Garrity lifts his head. Luca gasps. Anselm lies on his back in the grass, headlamp askew, staring up at the grey sky, bleeding from one forearm.

ANSELM
(laughing, appalled)
I'm still here.

GARRITY
(sprinting to him)
Don't move!

LUCA
Medic! MEDIC!

Garrity kneels, rips a sleeve, presses a field dressing to the gash. Luca stumbles through the grass, trips on a wire, falls. The radio flies from his hand. It lands in the weeds and crackles to life.

Luca scrambles for it, shaking, grinning, weeping. Garbled static resolves into a hiss of carrier tone.

LUCA
(into radio)
This is Fox Two! Anyone! We're alive!
